### canonicalization
- The operation cheminformatics performs instead of keeping the automorphisms is canonical labeling:
  choose a total order on the atoms by graph invariants, traverse, serialize. The first such algorithm is
  Morgan's, written in 1965 for Chemical Abstracts Service, and the lineage runs through Weininger,
  Weininger and Weininger's CANGEN (*J. Chem. Inf. Comput. Sci.* 29:97, 1989) to the InChI
  canonicalization.
- The field's own statement of what that costs is exact. Krotko, "Atomic ring invariant and Modified CANON
  extended connectivity algorithm for symmetry perception in molecular graphs and rigorous canonicalization
  of SMILES" (*J. Cheminform.* 12:48, 2020), opens with "The perception of the symmetry of atoms in
  molecular graphs is an important problem for chemoinformatics", and reports Ivanciuc's conclusion: "only
  algorithms with an explicit determination of the graph automorphisms (an isomorphism of a graph with
  itself is called an automorphism) can accurately determine all symmetry classes in complex" graphs.
- The standard does not exist. O'Boyle, "Towards a Universal SMILES representation" (*J. Cheminform.*
  4:22, 2012): "no standard exists to generate a canonical SMILES string", CANGEN "did not include a
  treatment of stereochemistry, one of the most difficult aspects of the problem", "There is no published
  canonical SMILES implementation for the general case", canonicalization has "many corner cases" and is in
  places a trade secret, and so "Each cheminformatics toolkit generates its own canonical SMILES". Grossman
  and co-authors (2005) showed the unique-SMILES algorithm failing to produce unique identifiers.
- The structural objection, stated from inside the field: "just identifying two graphs as isomorphic does
  not directly provide a UID."
- *Claude's gloss:* that sentence is §8.3's argument in the field's words. Deciding that two presentations
  are isomorphic is inhabiting `X ≃ Y`; a unique identifier is a chosen enumeration; and the one does not
  give the other, which is why the identifier has to be computed by a procedure whose corner cases differ
  between toolkits and are sometimes proprietary. Ivanciuc's conclusion closes the loop from the other
  side: the symmetry classes — the thing σ and the NMR signal counts are computed from — are only correct
  when the automorphisms are determined explicitly, which is the datum the set quotient discards and
  `GraphSym` keeps. §4.4's sorting is the model: build the answer over the identities the object carries
  rather than reading a chosen presentation as extra order. The repository's own work on canonical forms of
  sequences (`index-every-repeated-sequence`, `lib/repeats-kasai.cub`) is the same operation one dimension
  down.
**links:** molecular-graph · automorphism-is-physical · the-three-identities · tautomers · isomorphism-cost.

### isomorphism-cost
- The first objection to keeping the structure is that deciding graph identity is expensive. The complexity
  record: Babai, "Graph Isomorphism in Quasipolynomial Time" (arXiv:1512.03547, December 2015; STOC 2016),
  showed GI solvable in `exp((log n)^O(1))` time, with Johnson graphs "the only obstructions to effective
  canonical partitioning"; Helfgott found a flaw in the running-time analysis, Babai retracted the
  quasipolynomial claim on 4 January 2017, announced a correction on 9 January and published it on 19
  January, and Helfgott confirmed the fix, leaving a bound of `2^O((log n)^3)`. Earlier, Boppana and
  co-authors had placed GI in NP ∩ coAM, so it is not NP-complete unless the polynomial hierarchy
  collapses, and Babai, Erdős and Selkow showed GI easy on average.
- In practice the question is different again, and the practical literature answers it in chemistry's
  favour: molecular graphs are small, sparse and richly labeled by element and bond order, color refinement
  resolves almost all of them, and McKay and Piperno's `nauty`/`Traces` line of work ("Engineering an
  efficient canonical labeling tool for large and sparse graphs", 2007) is engineered for exactly that
  regime. The canonical-labeling algorithms compute the automorphism group on the way to the label rather
  than instead of it.
- The loop closes on itself: Grohe and Schweitzer's survey of the problem (*CACM*, November 2020) notes that
  color refinement is "one of the most basic ideas in graph isomorphism testing that has been reinvented
  several times; the oldest published version that we are aware of can be found in Morgan" — Morgan's 1965
  algorithm for Chemical Abstracts Service. They also note why the field prefers labels: "a canonical form γ
  yields an isomorphism test... In practical applications, canonical forms are often preferable over
  isomorphism tests."
- *Claude's gloss:* chemistry invented the practical subroutine of graph isomorphism testing and then
  discarded the output it produces. Keeping the automorphism group is not an added cost on top of
  canonicalization; it is the datum the canonicalizer computes and throws away, so the groupoid presentation
  is cheaper than the usual objection assumes and in the common case free. The preference for canonical
  forms over isomorphism tests is a real engineering fact and not an argument about identity: a label is a
  fast equality check, and the error is treating the check's output as the chemistry.
**links:** canonicalization · automorphism-is-physical · molecular-graph.

### automorphism-is-physical
- The order of a molecule's automorphism group is a quantity that appears in its thermodynamics. The
  symmetry number σ is the order of the symmetry group, and it corrects the overcounting of equivalent
  arrangements in the partition function; the rotational partition function divides by it, so σ enters the
  rotational entropy and through it every equilibrium constant and rate computed from first principles.
  Benzene's σ is 12 and water's is 2.
- The computational-chemistry literature computes σ from the graph: Vandewiele, Van de Vijver, Van Geem,
  Reyniers and Marin (*J. Comput. Chem.* 36(3):181–92, 2015) take "the automorphism group order of the graph
  associated with the molecule or transition state" as the starting point, over an augmented graph that
  accounts for both topology and stereocenters, and introduce label-stereoisomers — stereoisomers that arise
  after labeling homomorph substituents so they become distinguishable. Their stated motivation is fast
  screening of symmetry over large sets of molecules.
- The same automorphism orbits are the constitutionally equivalent atoms, and therefore the NMR equivalence
  classes: atoms in one orbit give one signal. The complete information about constitutionally equivalent
  atoms and bonds is contained in the automorphism group of the molecular graph.
- *Claude's gloss:* σ is a term in the entropy, so the set quotient that discards the automorphisms discards
  a physical quantity and the stored object can no longer produce it. That the field recomputes σ from the
  graph when it needs it is the point: the computation exists, the identifier cannot hold its result, and
  every downstream consumer re-derives or omits it. The groupoid presentation holds it by construction. This
  also gives the cheapest falsifiable demonstration available in the domain: predicted counts of distinct
  ¹H and ¹³C signals from orbit counts, checked against published spectra at scale — a physical prediction
  the standard representation cannot make, rather than an argument that the foundations are better.
**links:** molecular-graph · nmr-prediction · reaction-degeneracy · crystal-symmetry.

### chirality
- Chirality is not a property of the constitution graph. Two carbons each bearing four different
  substituents admit graph automorphisms that no rotation of three-dimensional space realizes, so the object
  is the graph together with an orientation assignment at its stereocenters, and identity is an equivalence
  that commutes with the orientation component — §2.2's point that a bare equivalence of total spaces
  forgets which inputs and outputs the witnesses relate.
- Enantiomers are the same graph with the orientation globally inverted; diastereomers with it partially
  inverted. A meso compound is the case where the inverting map lies inside the automorphism group, so the
  molecule is identified with its mirror image by an automorphism it already carries.
- Natural products average 6.2 chiral centers per molecule, against a much lower figure for synthetic
  drugs, so the stereo component carries most of the identity information in exactly the compounds whose
  structures are hardest to assign.
- *Claude's gloss:* meso detection in current toolkits is a procedural test over canonical labels; stated as
  membership of the inverting map in the automorphism group it is decidable by construction. Thalidomide is
  the standing cost of the registration systems of the time not distinguishing the two orientations of one
  graph.
**links:** molecular-graph · automorphism-is-physical · structure-revision · the-three-identities.

### tautomers
- Tautomers are isomers that convert into each other through chemical equilibrium — keto and enol forms, for
  the standard example. They are not one compound and not two unrelated ones, and the right object is a
  higher inductive type whose points are the individual structures and whose path constructors are the
  proton transfers with their conditions. That is §4.4's bag construction with different generators: points
  `emp`, `sng`, `un` there; here one point per structure, one path per transfer.
- The elimination discipline then does the work. §4.3: "A quotient identifies specified distinctions, and
  operations on it have to respect them; other observations may need another representation." Exact mass
  respects the paths and descends to the class. Dipole moment, ultraviolet absorption maximum, hydrogen-bond
  donor count, and the shape presented to a binding pocket do not respect them, and elimination into those
  targets does not typecheck without supplying what the path branches do, which is the ensemble population.
- The field's identity standard was built to be tautomer-invariant and is not. The InChI and its hashed
  InChIKey, developed at NIST and sanctioned by IUPAC, "were from the beginning (early 2000s) intended to be
  tautomer invariant. The way the InChI algorithm was coded, however, implemented tautomer invariance only
  partially." Standard InChI handles a limited range of tautomerism types, additional types are available
  only through non-standard options (`KET`, `15T`), and "It was recognized early on that important types of
  tautomerism are missing."
- The numbers are the standards body's own. Over the Cambridge Structural Database, 319,201 structures of
  which 203,108 are the tautomeric part, standard InChI's success rate is 26.25% and its strict success rate
  13.46%; over ChEMBL, 1,820,035 structures of which 1,578,290 tautomeric, 62.15% and 28.55%. IUPAC project
  #2012-023-2-800, "Redesign of Handling of Tautomerism for InChI V2" (Nicklaus, NCI), has been open since
  2012, and its rules were analyzed against nine databases totaling over 400 million non-unique structures.
  SMILES strings are neither designed to be nor in practice tautomer invariant. Martin has documented cases
  where a minor or less stable tautomer is the one found in a macromolecular binding site.
- *Claude's gloss:* an identifier intended to be invariant under a relation, implemented as invariant under
  part of it, with a redesign project open for over a decade, is the exact shape of a representation that
  asserts an identity its subject does not have. The HIT presentation cannot be written so as to make the
  substitution silently, because the path branches have to be supplied. Guanine's tautomers differ in their
  base pairing, which is a mutation mechanism, and a canonical-string treatment of the nucleobases has
  nowhere to put it.
**links:** the-three-identities · molecular-graph · binding-observation · structure-revision ·
interconversion-and-timescale.

### interconversion-and-timescale
- Tautomerism is one instance of a general fact that the carrier has to hold: whether two forms are one
  substance depends on how fast they interconvert relative to the observation, and the field's deepest
  case is thalidomide. The molecule has one stereocentre, at C-3 of the glutarimide ring, and the
  configuration is labile: enantiomerically pure thalidomide racemizes in aqueous buffer at pH 7.4 and
  37 °C with a half-life of 266 ± 10 minutes for (S) and 295 ± 24 for (R), and in human serum with a
  half-life of 21.8 ± 1.0 minutes for (S) and 32.9 for (R). The enantiomers were resolved in 1970, nine
  years after the withdrawal.
- The textbook account — (S) is the teratogen, (R) is the sedative, so a pure enantiomer would have been
  safe — does not survive those numbers, and the literature says so: the interconversion in vivo raises
  what the field calls the thalidomide paradox, that teratogenicity is not observed in animal experiments
  given (R) despite ready racemization, and the 1967 rabbit work found both enantiomers teratogenic. A
  2018 hypothesis explains the paradox by self-disproportionation — a fraction epimerises and precipitates
  as the (R/S) heterodimer while enantiomerically pure material stays in solution. Roughly 10,000 children
  were affected, with many more pregnancies lost.
- *Claude's gloss:* this is written in `chem/identity.cub` and it computes. `Interconv` carries the
  interconversions of a set of forms with their half-lives; `ic-within` says which an observation may
  identify — those that interconvert faster than the window the observation takes — so the identification
  is data rather than a convention. With thalidomide's serum half-lives as that data, `td-one-in-a-day`
  computes that over a day's exposure the two forms are one, and `td-two-in-ten-minutes` computes that
  over ten minutes they are two. Same molecule, same measurements, opposite verdicts, and both correct.
  So "are these the same substance" has no answer apart from the observation; a carrier that answers it
  once, for every purpose — which is what a canonical string does, and what InChI's tautomer rules do by
  fiat — answers it wrongly for most purposes. And the clinical form of that error is the belief that
  resolving the enantiomers would have prevented the harm: the body walks the path whatever the bottle
  contains.
**links:** tautomers · chirality · automorphism-is-physical · regulatory-identity · the-construction.

### organometallics
- The graph model has a boundary and the field knows where it is. SMILES admits four bond types — "Single,
  double, triple, and aromatic bonds are represented by the symbols –, =, #, and :" — and molecular
  inorganic chemistry exceeds that: d-orbital overlap allows bond orders higher than three, metal centers
  occur in many coordination environments, and (organo)metal compounds are often characterized by extensive
  electron delocalization and multi-centric bonding "which cannot be captured in simple Lewis formulas".
  Ferrocene, [Fe(η⁵–C₅H₅)₂], already defeats the cyclic-structure convention.
- InChI's treatment is to disconnect the metal bonds, and the consequence is stated in the open: "The
  connection of metal bonds is by definition disconnected which makes most inorganic InChIs meaningless at
  the moment." IUPAC Project 2009-040-2-800 on representation of organometallic and coordination compounds
  commenced in November 2010 and the working group is "working slowly towards a proposal"; the project's own
  risk assessment reads "Organometallic InChI fundamental to integrity and perception of InChI: reputational
  risk if left unaddressed – possible barrier to adoption". A 2023 workshop at RWTH Aachen took up inorganic
  stereochemistry, which the current version does not touch at all, and Yerin's stated requirements include
  procedures to determine configurationally inequivalent ligands and a bond model for delocalized ligands.
- *Claude's gloss:* this is the honest limit of the construction above and it should be stated as one. A
  graph with four bond types is a model of localized two-center bonding, and where the bonding is
  delocalized and multi-centric the graph is not the object — so identity there is not a graph identity, and
  no amount of keeping automorphisms fixes a carrier that is wrong. What the construction does supply is the
  place to put the right carrier: the paper's §5.4 schema holds a language's own syntax, judgments and
  semantics as data, so a bonding model with its own identity relation is a definition rather than a layer
  bolted onto a graph, and §4.3's discipline says which conclusions transport from the localized model to it
  and which do not. "Configurationally inequivalent ligands" is an orbit computation under the right group.
  Two decades of a working group moving slowly is what it looks like when the carrier is wrong and the
  identifier has to be patched instead.
**links:** molecular-graph · the-three-identities · canonicalization · crystal-symmetry.

### the-digest-has-an-error-bar
- The InChIKey is the hashed form used as a database key: 27 characters — a 14-character first block for the
  molecular skeleton, an 8-character second block for stereo, isotopic and (in non-standard keys) tautomeric
  isomerism, three flag characters and two dashes — computed as a truncated SHA-256 of the InChI string.
- Its designers publish its failure rate. Pletnev, Erin, McNaught, Blinov, Tchekhovskoi and Heller,
  "InChIKey collision resistance: an experimental testing" (*J. Cheminform.* 4:39, 2012), state the
  position: "due to the very essence of hash functions, collisions (the same InChIKey for different
  InChIs/structures) are unavoidable in very large collections", and "The appearance of collision itself does
  not compromise the signature as collision-free hashing is impossible; the only viable approach is to set
  and keep a reasonable level of collision resistance which is sufficient for typical applications." The
  theoretical estimate is about 2.2 × 10¹⁵ distinguishable keys, giving a 1.3% probability of a single
  first-block collision in a database of one billion compounds and 0.014% at one hundred million; the design
  goal was to avoid collisions up to several thousand isomers per skeleton. They tested the short second
  block exhaustively against all 67,108,864 stereoisomers of spongistatin I. The first InChIKey collision
  was reported in 2009.
- *Claude's gloss:* this is the cleanest possible confirmation of the first clause of (10.22). `digest(d₁) =
  digest(d₂)` does not imply that `d₁` and `d₂` are the same stored representation, and the field has
  published the probability that it fails to. The authors' position is correct on its own terms — a hash is
  a hash and they chose a defensible resistance level — and it is precisely why a hash match is evidence
  about a representation rather than an identity: the store can use it as a fast index and has to keep the
  relation it actually stands for alongside. A registration system that treats the key as the identity has
  adopted a 0.014% error rate on the question of whether two substances are the same, at a database size the
  CAS Registry passed in 2015.
**links:** the-three-identities · canonicalization · identity-authority · molecular-graph.

### identity-authority
- The field's working solution to entity resolution is an authority. The CAS Registry began in 1965 as a
  project to uniquely identify and track chemical substances; CAS, a division of the American Chemical
  Society, describes itself as "the world's authority for chemical information" and the Registry as "the
  only complete and accurate source of unique identifiers"; it registered its hundred-millionth substance on
  30 June 2015, about 75 million of those added in the preceding decade, with the records curated by CAS
  scientists from publicly disclosed literature.
- The identifiers are property. CAS Registry Numbers are copyright CAS/ACS, the license forbids compiling
  substantial lists, and when Antony Williams reported that CAS would not permit CAS numbers in Wikipedia
  curated from the official database, Murray-Rust's summary was: "They would make excellent identifiers for
  the semantic web, except that they are closed", with Wikipedia then holding between one and two thousand
  chemical substances, about 0.01% of the Registry. CAS Common Chemistry, opened in 2009 and expanded in
  2021, publishes about 500,000 substances — roughly half a percent of the Registry.
- The open identifier's own history runs the other way: InChI was developed by IUPAC and NIST from 2000 to
  2005 with non-proprietary format and algorithms, was LGPL through version 1.04, moved to a custom
  IUPAC-InChI Trust licence for 1.05 and 1.06, and has been MIT-licensed since 1.07 (1.07.5, February 2026),
  with the source now on GitHub.
- *Claude's gloss:* Murray-Rust's complaint that "there is no authority that catalogues chemistry and
  assigns open identifiers" is exact if the word *open* carries the weight: the authority exists and is
  closed, and the open alternative is an algorithm whose identity relation is the partial one documented
  above. Those are the only two options the field has had, and §10.8 is the third: definitions are
  content-addressed, each identity relation is recorded with its evidence and the strongest available is
  used for the operation, a peer sends a definition with its dependency closure and its proofs, and "No
  global namespace is needed for definition identity". Identity by authority and identity by evidence are
  different institutions, and the first is why a hundred million substances are legible only through a
  subscription. This is the same object as `data-liberation` in the workflow.
**links:** the-digest-has-an-error-bar · the-three-identities · canonicalization · workflow:
data-liberation.

### the-three-identities
- (10.22) applied: two records with equal digests are the same stored representation; two objects
  definitionally equal are the same object in the language; two objects related by `SemEq` are the same
  chemical entity under a stated relation. There is no single `SemEq` for chemistry, and the relation
  differs by case:
  - **tautomers** — a path, with conditions, under which only some properties descend;
  - **stereoisomers** — distinct objects, related by an equivalence that does not commute with the
    orientation component;
  - **salts, hydrates, solvates** — one shared active moiety observed in different solids, so a conclusion
    over the moiety's fiber persists while solubility and dosing conclusions do not;
  - **isotopologues** — the same graph with different vertex masses, physically distinct by the kinetic
    isotope effect: deutetrabenazine was approved in 2017 as its own drug because deuterium substitution
    slows its metabolism, a patentable and clinical distinction invisible to an object keyed on element
    identity alone. InChI has an Isotopologues working group, chaired by Moseley, for this reason;
  - **polymorphs** — identical composition and constitution, different solid, different behavior.
- *Claude's gloss:* the store records each relation with its evidence and uses the strongest available for
  the operation at hand, which is §10.8's rule. A hash match is evidence about a stored representation and
  nothing more, and the practice of treating it as chemical identity is why cross-database merging is manual
  and lossy in exactly these five cases. Murray-Rust's objection is the same one from inside the field: the
  identifier "is now being overused and badly used", and there is no authority that catalogues chemistry and
  assigns open identifiers, because the identity of many compounds is genuinely difficult.
